    Section(
      title: '+ What clinical findings in this case are consistent with intimate partner violence, and what are your documentation and assessment obligations?',
      content:
          '**Clinical Indicators of Intimate Partner Violence**:\n\n**1. Injury Pattern**:\n- **Inconsistent mechanism**: A fall down stairs causing a T10 burst fracture is plausible, but the patient initially reported being pushed, then recanted — classic IPV retraction pattern.\n- **Prior injuries with implausible mechanisms**: Three ED visits in 18 months — rib fracture (\'fell against countertop\'), orbital contusion (\'walked into door\'), forearm burns (\'cooking accident\'). Each at a different hospital (\'hospital shopping\' to avoid detection).\n- **Physical examination findings**: (a) Finger-pattern ecchymoses on the upper arm — virtually pathognomonic for gripping/grabbing. (b) Cigarette burn on hand — circular, uniform depth, consistent with deliberate burn. (c) Linear forearm burn — may be consistent with being held against a hot surface.\n\n**2. Behavioral Indicators**:\n- Avoids eye contact, whispered speech, flinches with male staff — hypervigilance consistent with chronic abuse.\n- Minimal therapy engagement, crying at night.\n- Self-blame: "It was my fault. I should not have made him angry" — internalizing the abuser\'s narrative.\n- Retracted initial truthful disclosure when partner arrived — fear of retaliation.\n\n**3. Pattern of Coercive Control**:\n- No independent bank account, phone, or car — financial and logistical control.\n- Isolated from friend for 2 years — social isolation is a hallmark of coercive control.\n- Partner calls nursing station 3-4 times daily, demands to visit, states he will \'take care of her\' — controlling behavior extending into the medical setting.\n\n**Documentation Obligations**:\n\n1. **Document findings objectively and in detail**:\n- Describe injuries precisely: location, size, shape, color, pattern. Use body diagrams.\n- Quote the patient\'s statements verbatim when possible (\'patient states ___\').\n- Record the inconsistency between stated mechanism and injury pattern.\n- Document prior ED visits and injury patterns.\n- **Do NOT write \'domestic violence\' as speculation** — document observable findings that raise concern and the patient\'s direct statements.\n\n2. **Photography**: With patient consent, photograph all injuries (including old scars and bruises) with a ruler for scale. These become part of the medical record and may be critical evidence if she later decides to pursue legal action.\n\n3. **Safety assessment**: Document current danger — is the patient safe in the hospital? Is the abuser attempting to access her? What is the discharge safety plan?\n\n4. **Mandatory reporting considerations**: Varies by state:\n- **All states**: Healthcare providers are mandatory reporters for child abuse and elder abuse.\n- **IPV in adults**: Only some states mandate reporting for adult IPV. Check state law. In states that do not mandate reporting, the patient\'s autonomy to decline reporting must be respected — but support and resources must be offered.\n- **This case**: She has declined to press charges. In a non-mandatory reporting state, document this decision. In a mandatory reporting state, report as required and inform the patient.\n- Regardless of reporting requirements, the **injuries were caused by an assault** (she reported being pushed), and assault is a crime that may trigger mandatory reporting of violent injuries in many states.',
