    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a rehabilitation and safety plan for an IPV survivor with a new SCI, including safe discharge planning.',
      content:
          '**Integrated Rehabilitation and Safety Plan**:\n\n**1. Physical Rehabilitation (Standard T10 ASIA A)**:\n- **Mobility**: Wheelchair skills training, transfer training (bed, toilet, car), wheelchair propulsion on varied surfaces.\n- **Strengthening**: Upper body progressive resistance program. Core stability with intact musculature.\n- **Neurogenic bladder**: CIC training — she must be fully independent. Every 4-6 hours. Teach her to recognize UTI symptoms.\n- **Neurogenic bowel**: Digital stimulation bowel program training. Schedule, dietary management, medications.\n- **Skin**: Pressure relief every 15-30 minutes in wheelchair. Daily skin inspection with mirror. Appropriate cushion.\n- **ADL independence**: Dressing, bathing, grooming, cooking from wheelchair level — all critical for independent living away from the abuser.\n\n**2. Psychological Trauma Treatment**:\n- **Trauma-informed care**: All staff educated — do not require detailed retelling of abuse, ask permission before touching, explain all procedures in advance, allow her to maintain control of interactions.\n- **Individual therapy**: Begin trauma-focused therapy with a psychologist experienced in IPV. CBT or CPT for PTSD. Start with safety and stabilization before processing trauma.\n- **Sertraline**: Continue and optimize dosing (currently 50 mg — therapeutic range for PTSD is 100-200 mg). Titrate as tolerated.\n- **Group therapy**: If available, IPV survivor group can be powerful. She has been isolated — connection with other survivors counters the abuser\'s narrative.\n\n**3. Safety Planning (Critical)**:\n\n**Immediate (during hospitalization)**:\n- **Restrict partner access**: Extend the safety hold. Work with security and hospital administration to ensure the partner cannot access the unit. He is NOT listed as next of kin or healthcare proxy.\n- **Separate phone line**: Provide her with a hospital phone that the partner does not know about. Help her contact her parents and her friend.\n- **Legal consultation**: Domestic violence advocate and legal aid referral. Discuss protection order options. She may not be ready — do not pressure, but ensure she knows her options.\n\n**Discharge (DO NOT DISCHARGE TO THE PARTNER)**:\n- **Accessible domestic violence shelter**: Work with DV advocacy organization to identify an accessible shelter (wheelchair-accessible). This is a significant challenge — many DV shelters are not accessible. Start planning early.\n- **If no accessible shelter available**: Accessible transitional housing, family placement (parents in another state may be an option if housing is accessible), or extended rehabilitation stay while housing is arranged.\n- **Financial**: Emergency Medicaid, SSI/SSDI application (she now qualifies with complete paraplegia), SNAP benefits, emergency cash assistance.\n- **Phone**: Obtain a prepaid phone in her name that the partner does not know about. Program crisis hotline numbers.\n- **Safety plan document**: Developed with DV advocate — specific steps if the partner finds her, emergency contacts, escape plan.\n\n**4. Independence as Protection**:\n- **Her rehabilitation IS her safety plan.** The more independent she is — in mobility, ADLs, finances, housing — the harder it is for the abuser to control her.\n- OT should specifically train: money management, phone use, emergency calls, online resources for DV survivors with disabilities.\n- **Driver rehabilitation**: Once she is ready, hand control driving evaluation. A car equals freedom and escape capability.\n\n**5. Disability-Specific DV Resources**:\n- Women with disabilities experience IPV at **2-3 times the rate** of non-disabled women.\n- Post-injury, she is MORE vulnerable — she may believe she cannot survive without the abuser.\n- **National Domestic Violence Hotline**: 1-800-799-7233 (has TTY and accessible options).\n- **Abused Deaf Women\'s Advocacy Services (ADWAS)** and **Disability Rights organizations** provide specialized DV resources.\n- **Independent Living Centers**: Can provide housing, advocacy, and peer support for women with new disabilities.',
