    Section(
      title: '+ How do you manage an abusive partner\'s attempts to access a patient within the hospital system, and what institutional protections exist?',
      content:
          '**Institutional Safety Protocols**:\n\n**1. Visitor Restriction and Extension**:\n- **Extend the safety hold**: Work with social work and hospital administration to extend the visitor restriction. This typically requires documentation of safety concern and the patient\'s expressed or implied wish not to have contact.\n- **Patient consent**: If the patient consents to restricting the partner, this is straightforward. If she is ambivalent (common in IPV), document the clinical basis for restriction (safety concern based on documented abuse pattern).\n- **Hospital security notification**: Provide security with the partner\'s name, physical description, and photograph if available. He should not be permitted past the front desk.\n- **Code word system**: Establish a code word with the patient — if the partner somehow reaches her by phone or in person, she can use the word to alert staff she needs help.\n\n**2. Legal Protections**:\n- **Protection order**: The DV advocate can help file an emergency protection order (EPO) or temporary restraining order (TRO). If granted, the partner is legally barred from contact.\n- **Hospital as private property**: The hospital can issue a trespass warning to the partner. If he returns after being warned, he can be arrested.\n- **Information restriction**: Place the patient on a \'confidential\' or \'no information\' status in the hospital system. If anyone calls asking if she is a patient, the hospital responds that they have no information. Her room number should not be accessible to non-clinical staff.\n\n**3. Communication Protocol**:\n- **Nursing station instructions**: All staff informed — do NOT provide any information about the patient to callers. Do not confirm or deny she is a patient. Do not accept gifts or deliveries from the partner.\n- **Chart access restriction**: Ensure the partner cannot access medical records (he is not the healthcare proxy). HIPAA protections apply.\n- **Caller ID**: If the partner\'s phone number is known, flag it at the nursing station.\n\n**4. Discharge Safety Systems**:\n- **Confidential discharge**: Do not include discharge destination in any records the partner could access. Use \'confidential address\' or \'safe location\' in discharge planning notes.\n- **Transportation**: Arrange discharge transportation that the partner cannot track — not a vehicle he would recognize.\n- **Medical records**: Ensure new address/phone (at shelter or safe location) is not linked to records at the old address.\n\n**5. Multidisciplinary Safety Team**:\n- Convene: PM&R physician, social worker, DV advocate, hospital security, nursing leadership, patient (when she is ready).\n- Develop a written safety plan that covers the remainder of her hospitalization and the transition to a safe discharge location.\n- Assign one social worker as the primary coordinator — too many contacts increases the risk of information leaks.\n\n**6. Staff Safety**:\n- Abusive partners sometimes become aggressive toward staff who block access. Security presence when he is known to be at the hospital.\n- De-escalation protocols for staff who interact with him by phone or in person.\n- Staff should NOT engage in confrontation — refer all interactions to security.',
