    Section(
      title: '+ How do you handle a phone call from the patient\'s abusive partner who is asserting his role as her caregiver and requesting involvement in her discharge?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Do not confirm or deny the patient is at the hospital.**\n"Thank you for calling. Due to patient privacy laws, I am unable to confirm or deny whether any individual is a patient at this facility, and I cannot discuss any patient\'s medical information without their written authorization."\n\n**2. If he presses (which he will):**\n"I understand your concern. Our policy applies to all patients and all callers. If someone you know is receiving care here and wishes to include you in their care, they can authorize that through their care team. I cannot provide any further information."\n\n**3. Do not engage with his narrative.**\n- He will say she needs him, she has no one else, he has been taking care of her.\n- Do NOT argue, correct, or challenge him on the phone. Do not say "we know what you did" or "she told us you pushed her." This could escalate his behavior and endanger the patient.\n- Do not provide any information about her condition, her injury, her discharge date, or her location.\n\n**4. If he becomes threatening or aggressive:**\n"I hear that you are upset. If you feel you need to speak with someone about a patient concern, you can contact our Patient Relations department at [number]. If you come to the hospital, please check in at the front desk. I am going to end this call now."\n- Document the call, including his exact words, tone, and any threats.\n- Notify hospital security immediately.\n- Notify the social worker and DV advocate.\n\n**5. After the call — document and act:**\n- Document the call verbatim in the medical record.\n- Notify the patient that her partner called and what he said — she has the right to know. Ask her how she wants to proceed.\n- Inform the care team — this is an escalation. His language (\'picking her up,\' \'she has nobody,\' \'who will take care of her\') frames her disability as a reason she needs him. This is **weaponizing her disability as a tool of control** — a recognized IPV tactic when a partner becomes disabled.\n- Increase security awareness. Consider whether the call pattern constitutes harassment (repeated unwanted calls) that could support a protection order.\n\n**6. Discuss with the patient (separately, privately):**\n"I want to tell you about a phone call I received from [partner\'s name]. He called asking about you and said he wants to be involved in your discharge. He said you need him because of your injury. I want to ask you: Is that what you want? Because I want you to know something — you are learning to do everything independently. You do not need anyone to take care of you. You are going to be able to catheterize yourself, manage your bowel program, transfer, dress, cook, and live on your own. Your injury does not make you dependent on him or anyone else. You have options, and I will help you see every one of them."\n\n**Key Principles**: HIPAA compliance — do not confirm or deny the patient is present. Do not engage with the abuser\'s framing. Do not provide information that could be used to control or locate the patient. Document everything. Inform the patient of all contacts. Counter the narrative that her disability makes her dependent on him. Safety of the patient and staff is paramount.',
